I have a `main.cpp` file that embeds the Python interpreter into C++. I'm trying to print an object in Python and later access its attributes that were defined in C++. However, I'm encountering an error when trying to cast a C++ object to a Python object. I followed the instructions in the documentation on casting back and forth, but the error persists. The error message states: "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'pybind11::cast_error' what(): make_tuple(): unable to convert argument of type 'object' to Python object". How can I resolve this issue?

Here is my `main.cpp` code:

```cpp
// Embeding the interpreter into c++
// https://pybind11.readthedocs.io/en/master/advanced/embedding.html

#include <pybind11/embed.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Define namespace for pybind11
namespace py = pybind11;

class Vehiclee
{ 
    // Access specifier 
    public: 

    // Data Members 
    int vehicle_id;
    std::string vehicle_name; 
    std::string vehicle_color;

    // Member Functions() 
    void printname() 
    { 
       std::cout << "Vehicle id is: " << vehicle_id; 
       std::cout << "Vehicle name is: " << vehicle_name; 
       std::cout << "Vehicle color is: " << vehicle_color; 
    } 
}; 

int main() {
    // Initialize the python interpreter
    py::scoped_interpreter python;

    // Import all the functions from scripts by file name in the working directory
    py::module simpleFuncs = py::module::import("simpleFuncs");   

    // Test if C++ objects can be passed into python functions
    Vehiclee car1;
    car1.vehicle_id = 1234;
    car1.vehicle_name = "VehicleName";
    car1.vehicle_color = "red";
    py::object car2 = py::cast(car1);   // <-- PROBLEM
    simpleFuncs.attr("simplePrint")(car2);

    return 0;
}
```

I also have a `simpleFuncs.py` file:

```python
def simplePrint(argument):
    print(argument)
```

I'm using CMake to compile and run the code. Here is my `CMakeLists.txt`:

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.0)
project(wrapper)
add_subdirectory(pybind11)
add_executable(wrapper main.cpp)
target_link_libraries(wrapper PRIVATE pybind11::embed)
```

I've cloned the pybind11 repository, ran `cmake .`, and then `make`. Compilation works fine, but when I execute the code, I get the aforementioned error. How can I resolve this issue? Thank you in advance.

To resolve the casting issue, you need to add binding code for the `Vehiclee` class and import the corresponding module. The `py::cast` call is not necessary in this case.

You can add the binding code for `Vehiclee` using the `PYBIND11_EMBEDDED_MODULE` macro. Here's an updated version of your code:

```cpp
// Embeding the interpreter into c++
// https://pybind11.readthedocs.io/en/master/advanced/embedding.html

#include <pybind11/embed.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Define namespace for pybind11
namespace py = pybind11;

class Vehiclee
{
  // Access specifier
public:

  // Data Members
  int vehicle_id;
  std::string vehicle_name;
  std::string vehicle_color;

  // Member Functions()
  void printname()
  {
    std::cout << "Vehicle id is: " << vehicle_id;
    std::cout << "Vehicle name is: " << vehicle_name;
    std::cout << "Vehicle color is: " << vehicle_color;
  }
};

PYBIND11_EMBEDDED_MODULE(embeded, module){
  py::class_<Vehiclee> animal(module, "Vehiclee");
}

int main() {
  // Initialize the python interpreter
  py::scoped_interpreter python;

  // Import all the functions from scripts by file name in the working directory
  auto simpleFuncs = py::module::import("simpleFuncs");
  auto embeded = py::module::import("embeded");

  // Test if C++ objects can be passed into python functions
  Vehiclee car1;
  car1.vehicle_id = 1234;
  car1.vehicle_name = "VehicleName";
  car1.vehicle_color = "red";

  simpleFuncs.attr("simplePrint")(car1);

  return 0;
}
```

